---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best VR Treadmill (2026): Five Home Options, Honestly Compared";
const description =
  "The best VR treadmill for home use in 2026: the Virtuix Omni One range and KAT Walk C2 series compared on price, headset support, space and limits, and why none is a fitness treadmill in the usual sense.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "virtuix-omni-one",
    name: "Virtuix Omni One",
    category: "Complete standalone VR treadmill system",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=VR+treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,500–$3,000",
    priceBounds: {"low":2500,"high":3000},
    specs: "Includes customised Pico 4 Ultra headset · users 4 ft 4 in to 6 ft 4 in, up to 250 lb · 48 x 60 x 48 in., 150 lb",
    pros: [
      "The only complete system here: treadmill, headset, controllers and trackers in one box",
      "No gaming PC needed",
      "Compact, about four feet across, with built-in wheels",
      "30-day refund guarantee and 12-month home warranty by Virtuix’s listing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The most expensive option here",
      "Games come from Virtuix’s own store; the bundled headset is not your choice",
      "A 250 lb user limit and a 6 ft 4 in maximum height",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For people who want the most finished, least fiddly VR treadmill experience and do not already own a headset or gaming PC.",
    description: "The Omni One is Virtuix's consumer treadmill, and in its full form it is the only option here that needs nothing else. Virtuix's store lists it at $2,995, or $104 a month, with a customised Pico 4 Ultra headset, controllers, overshoes, foot trackers and a cleaning kit, a 30-day refund guarantee and a 12-month home warranty; it launched at $2,595 with a Pico headset, according to 2024 coverage, and Virtuix has run $500-off codes since, so our band runs from $2,500 to $3,000. Virtuix lists users from 4 feet 4 inches to 6 feet 4 inches and up to 250 lb, and gives the unit as 48 by 60 by 48 inches and 150 lb. One hands-on reviewer was walking, jogging and running comfortably after about 15 minutes; others found the first hour awkward and compared the feel to walking on ice. Games come from Virtuix's Omni One store. It is a gaming device, not a fitness treadmill, and it ships to the US and Canada only.",
  },
  {
    id: "virtuix-omni-one-for-quest",
    name: "Virtuix Omni One for Quest",
    category: "VR treadmill for Meta Quest headsets",
    badge: "Best for Quest Owners",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=VR+treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $2,600",
    priceBounds: {"low":2600,"high":2600},
    specs: "Works with Meta Quest 2, 3 and 3S · users up to 250 lb · no headset included",
    pros: [
      "Uses the Quest you already own instead of a second headset",
      "Official Meta support, and sold through Meta’s accessory store as well as Virtuix",
      "Cheaper than the full Omni One, by about $400 at list",
      "Launched with a bundle of compatible Quest games",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Not every Quest game supports it yet",
      "Still a large outlay for a VR accessory",
      "The same 250 lb limit and sliding gait as the full Omni One",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For Meta Quest owners who play free-movement games and want to walk and run in them rather than use a thumbstick.",
    description: "Omni One for Quest launched in June 2026 as the first VR treadmill with official Meta Quest support. Road to VR, TechSpot and Virtuix all give the price as $2,595, and Virtuix's store listed it at that figure when we checked, with a monthly plan also offered. Virtuix's listing says it works with Quest 2, Quest 3 and Quest 3S headsets; Road to VR reported Quest 2 and 3 support at launch and named optimised games including VAIL, Forefront and Star Trek: Infection, with four titles bundled free for a limited time. It uses the same treadmill as the full Omni One, so the same 250 lb user limit and the same learning curve apply, but you supply the headset. Road to VR's one real caveat was that not all Quest games are supported, so check the titles you care about first. Launch coverage also pointed out the obvious: at $2,595 it costs several times as much as the headset it works with. Virtuix ships it to the US, Canada, the EU and the UK.",
  },
  {
    id: "virtuix-omni-one-core",
    name: "Virtuix Omni One Core",
    category: "VR treadmill for PC VR and SteamVR",
    badge: "Best Omni for PC VR",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=VR+treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,500–$2,600",
    priceBounds: {"low":2500,"high":2600},
    specs: "Treadmill only, for PC VR headsets and SteamVR games · users up to 250 lb · overshoes and foot trackers included",
    pros: [
      "Works with a wide range of PC VR headsets you may already own",
      "Access to compatible SteamVR games rather than one store",
      "Same compact Omni One base with built-in wheels",
      "Sold in the US, Canada, the EU and the UK",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Needs a VR-ready gaming PC as well as a headset",
      "Costs much more than the KAT Walk C2 series, which also targets PC VR",
      "Games must support free movement; PSVR2 is not supported",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For PC VR players who want the Omni One platform and already own a capable headset and PC.",
    description: "The Omni One Core is the Omni One treadmill without a headset, for people who already play on PC. Virtuix's store listed it at $2,495, or $87 a month, when we checked; Yanko Design and TweakTown gave $2,595 at its December 2024 launch, and in Europe it sells for €2,995 or £2,795 through Unbound XR, according to Auganix and Virtuix's own announcement. Coverage of the launch lists the treadmill, overshoes, foot trackers and a Bluetooth adapter in the box, with no headset. Virtuix's Omni Connect app links it to a PC and translates your movement into game input for supported SteamVR titles, and Virtuix lists a wide range of PC VR headsets as compatible, but not PSVR2. The user limits are the same as the full Omni One. The question with the Core is value: it costs well over the KAT Walk C2 Plus, which targets the same PC VR buyer. You are paying for Virtuix's platform, its compact design and its software rather than for extra hardware.",
  },
  {
    id: "kat-walk-c2-plus-enhanced",
    name: "KAT Walk C2 Plus Enhanced",
    category: "PC VR treadmill with haptics and seat module",
    badge: "Best Value for PC VR",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=KAT+Walk+C2+Plus+VR+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,200–$1,700",
    priceBounds: {"low":1200,"high":1700},
    specs: "Users up to about 286 lb (130 kg) · 49.2 x 49.2 x 62 in., 57 kg · PC VR headsets; Quest via Nexus adapter",
    pros: [
      "Roughly half the price of the Omni One Core for the same PC VR buyer",
      "Built-in haptic feedback and a seat module for seated play",
      "Broad PC VR headset support, and Quest support through an optional adapter",
      "Higher user weight limit than the Omni One",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Needs a VR-ready PC unless you add the Nexus adapter for a Quest",
      "Pricing depends heavily on rolling promotions, so the real price is hard to pin down",
      "Sources disagree on the supported height range",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For PC VR players who want a capable omnidirectional treadmill without paying Omni One prices.",
    description: "KAT VR's C2 series is the main alternative to Virtuix, and the C2 Plus Enhanced is its flagship home model. KAT's store listed it at $1,499 to $1,697 depending on options when we checked, with a promotion code taking it to $1,199, and Knoxlabs, an authorised retailer, lists it from $1,499. Knoxlabs gives it a footprint of 49.2 by 49.2 inches, a height of about 62 inches, a weight of 57 kg and a user weight limit below 286 lb, or 130 kg, which a university lab's equipment page and KAT's own blog repeat. The supported height range differs between sources, so we have left it out. Over the C2 Core it adds haptic feedback, a seat module for seated play and LED lighting, and KAT claims it runs about 37 percent quieter. Knoxlabs lists support for most PC VR headsets, Quest through KAT's Nexus adapter or a PC link, and only limited PSVR support. KAT's store was offering extended warranty terms as part of a promotion; get them in writing.",
  },
  {
    id: "kat-walk-c2-core-enhanced",
    name: "KAT Walk C2 Core Enhanced",
    category: "Entry-level PC VR treadmill",
    badge: "Lowest Price",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=KAT+Walk+C2+Core+VR+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$800–$1,500",
    priceBounds: {"low":800,"high":1500},
    specs: "Users up to about 286 lb (130 kg) · PC VR headsets; Quest via Nexus adapter · seat module optional",
    pros: [
      "The lowest price of any home VR treadmill we could confirm",
      "Same 286 lb class user limit as the C2 Plus",
      "Optional seat module and Nexus adapter let you add features later",
      "Same headset compatibility as the C2 Plus",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No haptics, LED lighting or built-in wheels, and louder than the Plus by KAT’s account",
      "Needs a VR-ready PC unless you add the Nexus adapter",
      "Little independent coverage specific to the Core",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For PC VR players who want to try omnidirectional movement for the least money, and can live without haptics.",
    description: "The C2 Core Enhanced is the stripped-back version of KAT's C2, and it is the cheapest route into a home VR treadmill we could confirm. KAT's store listed it at $999 on its own, $1,098 with the Nexus adapter for Quest, and up to $1,497 with the seat module and adapter, with promotion codes advertising $799 bundles when we checked; our band reflects that spread. It shares the C2 platform, so a university lab running a C2 and Knoxlabs give the same user limit of below 286 lb, or 130 kg. KAT's own comparison says the Core lacks the Plus's haptic feedback, LED lighting and built-in wheels, comes without the seat module unless you add it, and is louder. Independent hands-on coverage is mostly of the original C2; VRScout found it took a couple of hours to set up. If you are not sure VR locomotion is for you, this is the lowest-cost way to find out, but it still needs a VR-ready PC or a Quest with the adapter.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best VR Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-vr-treadmills/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best VR treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a complete system that needs no PC, the Virtuix Omni One, which comes with its own headset. For Meta Quest owners, the Omni One for Quest. For PC VR players on a budget, the KAT Walk C2 Plus Enhanced or the cheaper C2 Core. All are gaming devices rather than fitness treadmills, and prices move a lot with promotions.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a VR treadmill cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 14 October 2026, home VR treadmills ranged from about $800 for a promotional KAT Walk C2 Core bundle to about $3,000 for a complete Virtuix Omni One with headset. The Omni One for Quest was $2,595, and the Omni One Core $2,495. Commercial models such as the KAT Walk Mini S cost far more.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a VR treadmill good exercise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be active, depending on the game, but it is not a fitness treadmill. There is no belt, motor, set pace or incline; you slide your feet on a low-friction base while a harness holds you. If exercise is the main goal, a conventional treadmill does more for less money. A VR treadmill makes sense if you already play VR and want to move physically.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do VR treadmills work with Meta Quest?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some do. Virtuix's Omni One for Quest has official Meta support for Quest 2, 3 and 3S by Virtuix's listing, though not every Quest game supports it. The KAT Walk C2 series can work with a Quest through KAT's optional Nexus adapter or through a PC link. PlayStation VR2 is not supported by either maker's current models.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you buy an Infinadeck for home use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not as of October 2026, as far as we could find. Infinadeck's motorised omnidirectional treadmill is sold to enterprise, medical and government customers with no published price, and coverage suggests a cost far beyond consumer products. Its maker has talked about a consumer version but has not announced one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does a VR treadmill need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The base is about four feet across — Virtuix gives the Omni One as 48 by 60 inches, and Knoxlabs gives the KAT C2 Plus as 49.2 inches square. Add clear space around it for your arms and controllers, and check your ceiling height against the maker's supported height range, especially in a basement.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-14",
      dateModified: "2026-10-14",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-vr-treadmills/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-14",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-14",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best vr treadmill",
      "vr treadmill for home",
      "omnidirectional treadmill",
      "virtuix omni one",
      "kat walk c2",
      "vr treadmill price",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestvrtreadmills" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestvrtreadmills)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best VR Treadmill <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Five Home Options, Honestly Compared</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A VR treadmill lets you walk, run and turn inside a virtual world while staying in one spot. The home models we could confirm on sale in the US come from two companies, in five main configurations. Here is what each costs, which headsets it works with, how much room it needs — and why none of them is a fitness treadmill in the sense the rest of this site means.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Options
          </a>
          <a href="#not-a-treadmill" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Is It a Real Treadmill?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 14, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.5k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not used or tested
          these machines. Prices, configurations, headset compatibility, user limits and dimensions
          were checked on 14 October 2026 against each maker's own store and product pages
          (Virtuix and KAT VR) and cross-checked against at least one independent source — Road to
          VR, TechSpot, Auganix, Yanko Design and TweakTown coverage, hands-on impressions from Road
          to VR, Tom's Guide, GameSpace and VRScout, an authorised retailer (Knoxlabs) and a
          university lab's equipment page. Where sources disagreed, as they do on KAT's supported
          height range, the figure is left out and the description says so. Prices for these
          machines move a lot with promotions and bundles, so we give dated bands rather than a
          single figure; confirm the price, what is in the box and the warranty before ordering. We
          searched cpsc.gov for Virtuix and KAT VR and found no recall or warning. These are gaming
          and VR locomotion devices, not exercise equipment in the usual sense, and nothing here is
          fitness or medical advice. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on
          this page; it does not change which products we list or what we say about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Overall:</span>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Virtuix Omni One</a>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best for Quest Owners:</span>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one-for-quest" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Virtuix Omni One for Quest</a>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one-for-quest" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Omni for PC VR:</span>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one-core" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Virtuix Omni One Core</a>
            <a href="#virtuix-omni-one-core" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Value for PC VR:</span>
            <a href="#kat-walk-c2-plus-enhanced" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">KAT Walk C2 Plus Enhanced</a>
            <a href="#kat-walk-c2-plus-enhanced" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Lowest Price:</span>
            <a href="#kat-walk-c2-core-enhanced" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">KAT Walk C2 Core Enhanced</a>
            <a href="#kat-walk-c2-core-enhanced" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 5 Best VR Treadmills for Home Use</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#not-a-treadmill" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Why a VR Treadmill Is Not a Fitness Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#headsets-games" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Headsets, PCs and Games</a>
          <a href="#omni-vs-kat" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Omni One vs KAT Walk C2: The Real Differences</a>
          <a href="#total-cost" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What a VR Treadmill Really Costs</a>
