        <div id="not-a-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why a VR Treadmill Is Not a Fitness Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            This site is about treadmills for exercise, so it is worth being direct: a VR treadmill
            is a locomotion controller for virtual reality. It can make you move, sometimes hard,
            but it is built for games, not training, and it differs from a motorised treadmill in
            almost every way that matters for fitness.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li><strong>Nothing moves under you.</strong> There is no belt and no motor. You slide your
            feet across a low-friction, slightly concave base while a harness or waist ring holds you
            in place. Reviewers who have tried the Omni One describe the feel as closer to gliding
            than striding — like walking on slippery ice, or in snowshoes.</li>
            <li><strong>No set pace, no incline.</strong> You cannot dial in 6 mph at 5 percent and hold
            it. Your speed in the game follows how you move, and the effort depends on the game.</li>
            <li><strong>The gait is different.</strong> The sliding motion uses your legs and hips
            differently from walking or running on a belt or the road, so it does not train you for
            either in the way a treadmill does.</li>
            <li><strong>There is a learning curve.</strong> Hands-on reviewers report feeling awkward at
            first, needing anywhere from about 15 minutes to several hours before walking, turning and
            moving backwards feel natural.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower user limits.</strong> The Omni One supports users up to 250 lb and the KAT
            C2 series up to about 286 lb, below many home treadmills.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What a VR treadmill does well is presence. Turning your body to look around, walking
            down a corridor and breaking into a run when something chases you all feel more natural
            than pushing a thumbstick, and that is what buyers are paying for. One reviewer who
            spent several hours on the Omni One found the first hour awkward but the fifth
            dramatically different, so judge it after a week, not after a single session.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that makes a VR treadmill a bad buy. For the right person — someone who already
            spends hours in VR games and wants to move physically rather than push a joystick — it is
            a genuine upgrade, and the activity can be vigorous. But if your goal is fitness, a
            conventional treadmill will do more for less money, and our guides to
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmills</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for Zwift</a>
            cover the interactive end of that market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="headsets-games" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Headsets, PCs and Games</h2>
<p>
            A VR treadmill is only as useful as the headset and games it works with, and this is
            where the configurations differ most.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Standalone, no PC.</strong> The full Omni One ships with a customised Pico 4
            Ultra headset and controllers and runs games from Virtuix's own Omni One store. It is the
            only option here that needs nothing else, and the only one tied to a single store and
            headset.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Meta Quest.</strong> Omni One for Quest works with Quest 2, Quest 3 and Quest 3S
            headsets by Virtuix's listing and with Quest games that support it; Road to VR noted at
            launch that not all Quest games are supported yet. The KAT Walk C2 series can work with
            a Quest through KAT's Nexus adapter, sold as an option, or through a PC using Link, Air
            Link or Virtual Desktop, according to Knoxlabs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>PC VR and SteamVR.</strong> The Omni One Core and both KAT Walk C2 models work
            with PC VR headsets and SteamVR games. That means you need a VR-ready PC as well as the
            headset, which is a substantial extra cost if you do not already have one. Virtuix's
            Omni Connect app links the treadmill to the PC over Bluetooth and stands in for
            controller movement input, according to its product coverage, so games need to support
            free movement rather than teleporting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>PlayStation VR2</strong> is not supported by the Omni One, by Virtuix's page, and
            Knoxlabs lists it as not yet supported by the KAT C2.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Subscriptions.</strong> Virtuix sells an Omni Online membership, listed on its
            store at $84 to $140 a year, which it describes as a premium service with free games and
            online multiplayer. We could not confirm from Virtuix's pages whether any features you
            would consider essential sit behind it; ask before you buy. We did not find a
            subscription requirement for the KAT C2 series.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="omni-vs-kat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Omni One vs KAT Walk C2: The Real Differences</h2>
<p>
            With only two makers in the home market, most buyers end up choosing between them. The
            differences are less about the walking surface than about everything around it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price.</strong> KAT's C2 series costs roughly half as much as the Omni One range
            at list, and less again on promotion. If you already own a PC VR setup, the C2 Plus
            Enhanced does broadly the same job as the Omni One Core for about $1,000 less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ecosystem.</strong> Virtuix sells a complete, closed system with its own headset
            and game store, a Quest version with official Meta support, and a PC version. KAT sells
            hardware for the PC VR world, with an adapter for Quest. If you want something that works
            without tinkering, Virtuix's full system is the simpler path; if you like choosing your
            own headset and games, KAT's approach is more open and cheaper.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>User limits.</strong> The KAT C2 series supports heavier users, up to about 286
            lb against the Omni One's 250 lb. Virtuix publishes a clear height range, 4 feet 4 inches
            to 6 feet 4 inches; KAT's supported range differs between sources, so ask KAT directly if
            you are near either end.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint.</strong> The two bases are similar, around four feet across. The Omni
            One and the C2 Plus have wheels; KAT says the C2 Core does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pricing clarity.</strong> Virtuix's prices are fairly stable, with occasional
            discount codes. KAT's store runs near-constant promotions and bundles, which makes the
            real price hard to compare; work out exactly what is in the box at the price you are
            quoted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="total-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What a VR Treadmill Really Costs</h2>
<p>
            The treadmill is only part of the bill. Worked out from the prices we saw on 14 October
            2026, the cheapest complete setup for each option looks roughly like this.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li><strong>Omni One:</strong> about $3,000 all in, including the headset, or less with a
            discount code. Nothing else is needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Omni One for Quest:</strong> $2,595 plus a Quest headset if you do not have one.
            Virtuix's own store lists the Quest 3S at $349 and the Quest 3 at $599.</li>
            <li><strong>KAT Walk C2 Core with Nexus:</strong> $1,098 at list, or less on promotion,
            plus a Quest. This is the cheapest complete route we found, at around $1,450 with a Quest
            3S before promotions.</li>
            <li><strong>Omni One Core or KAT Walk C2 Plus for PC VR:</strong> $2,495 or about $1,500
            respectively, plus a PC VR headset and a VR-ready PC. If you own neither, that can double
            the cost.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Add any subscription you decide you want, spare overshoes or shoe parts, and the games
            themselves, many of which are paid. None of this is unreasonable for a dedicated VR
            player, but it is a lot more than the headline price suggests, and far more than a
            capable home treadmill for exercise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Space, Ceiling and Setup</h2>
<p>
            A VR treadmill needs less floor than you might expect, but more space around it than its
            footprint suggests, because your arms, controllers and occasionally your whole body move
            beyond its edge.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint.</strong> Virtuix gives the Omni One as 48 by 60 by 48 inches and 150
            lb, describing a base about four feet across, with built-in wheels. Knoxlabs gives the
            KAT Walk C2 Plus as 49.2 by 49.2 inches and about 62 inches tall, at 57 kg. Leave clear
            space around either for your arms and controllers, and keep it away from walls, shelves,
            lights and anything fragile.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling.</strong> The support arm or frame, plus a tall user with arms raised,
            needs headroom. A university lab that runs a KAT C2 notes its own low ceiling may reduce
            the usable height range, which is a useful warning for basements. Check the maker's
            height range against your ceiling before ordering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor and weight.</strong> These are not as heavy as a commercial treadmill, but
            150 lb plus a moving user is a real load. A firm, level floor matters, and on carpet you
            may want a hard mat beneath. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill space requirements</a>
            covers the general principles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Setup.</strong> VRScout found the KAT C2 took a couple of hours to get running.
            Expect to spend time on pairing shoes or foot trackers, calibrating, and setting up
            games, and then several sessions learning to move.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Look Elsewhere</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want to get fit,</strong> buy a treadmill. A conventional machine gives you
            pace, incline, distance and heart-rate training for a fraction of the price, and our
            guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills</a>
            is the place to start. If boredom is the problem, our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-make-treadmill-running-less-boring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making treadmill running less boring</a>
            covers scenic and game-based apps that work on ordinary machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are over about 250 lb,</strong> the Omni One is ruled out by its limit, and
            the KAT C2 series' roughly 286 lb limit leaves little margin for many people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you do not already play VR regularly,</strong> try VR first. Some people find
            VR uncomfortable or nauseating, and we have not seen independent evidence that a VR
            treadmill prevents that. Spending $1,000 to $3,000 on a locomotion device before you know
            you enjoy VR is a large bet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you play mostly seated or teleport-based games,</strong> a VR treadmill adds
            little. It earns its keep in games built around walking and running through large
            worlds.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have balance, joint or heart problems,</strong> talk to a doctor before
            using any VR locomotion device. The harness holds you upright, but you are moving with
            your vision fully inside a headset.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best VR treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a complete system that needs no PC, the Virtuix Omni One, which comes with its own headset. For Meta Quest owners, the Omni One for Quest. For PC VR players on a budget, the KAT Walk C2 Plus Enhanced or the cheaper C2 Core. All are gaming devices rather than fitness treadmills, and prices move a lot with promotions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a VR treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 14 October 2026, home VR treadmills ranged from about $800 for a promotional KAT Walk C2 Core bundle to about $3,000 for a complete Virtuix Omni One with headset. The Omni One for Quest was $2,595, and the Omni One Core $2,495. Commercial models such as the KAT Walk Mini S cost far more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a VR treadmill good exercise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be active, depending on the game, but it is not a fitness treadmill. There is no belt, motor, set pace or incline; you slide your feet on a low-friction base while a harness holds you. If exercise is the main goal, a conventional treadmill does more for less money. A VR treadmill makes sense if you already play VR and want to move physically.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do VR treadmills work with Meta Quest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do. Virtuix's Omni One for Quest has official Meta support for Quest 2, 3 and 3S by Virtuix's listing, though not every Quest game supports it. The KAT Walk C2 series can work with a Quest through KAT's optional Nexus adapter or through a PC link. PlayStation VR2 is not supported by either maker's current models.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you buy an Infinadeck for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as of October 2026, as far as we could find. Infinadeck's motorised omnidirectional treadmill is sold to enterprise, medical and government customers with no published price, and coverage suggests a cost far beyond consumer products. Its maker has talked about a consumer version but has not announced one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does a VR treadmill need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The base is about four feet across — Virtuix gives the Omni One as 48 by 60 inches, and Knoxlabs gives the KAT C2 Plus as 49.2 inches square. Add clear space around it for your arms and controllers, and check your ceiling height against the maker's supported height range, especially in a basement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a VR Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from expecting a VR treadmill to be a treadmill, or forgetting what else it needs.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one as a fitness machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A VR treadmill has no belt, set pace or incline, and its sliding gait is not the same as walking or running. If fitness is the goal, a conventional treadmill gives you more training for less money; a VR treadmill is for people who want to move inside VR games.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the PC and headset</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Only the full Omni One includes a headset and needs nothing else. The Omni One Core and both KAT C2 models need a PC VR headset and a VR-ready PC, or a Quest with the right adapter or version. Price the whole setup, not just the treadmill.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking your games</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">These treadmills replace thumbstick movement, so they suit games with free locomotion. Not every game is supported, and Road to VR noted that not all Quest titles work with Omni One for Quest yet. Check the specific games you play before buying.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the user limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Omni One supports users up to 250 lb and 6 feet 4 inches; the KAT C2 series up to about 286 lb. Both are lower than most home treadmills, so check the limits, and your ceiling height, against the tallest and heaviest person who will use it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

